\tag{fdip}
\taxon{Discussion}
\title{Two masses on one torus}
\date{2026-11-27}

\p{At mass #{1/a} the [overlap index](fdip-0153) is read from positive and negative subspaces of the regular Hermitian Wilson operator (\ref{fdip-0155}). Now mark two strips on the torus and put a different constant mass on each. A charged field can still hop from one strip into the other. How do the mass function and the two oriented sets of plaquettes enter a comparison with the old count?}
